</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table A2: </span>Division Boundaries by Difficulty</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A1.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem Difficulty.</span> We sort all task difficulty scores and split them into three equal-sized buckets by taking the empirical one-third and two-thirds cut points. The problem difficulty distribution is listed in Table <a href="#A1.T3" title="Table A3 ‣ A.3 Metadata Collection ‣ Appendix A Dataset Construction ‣ LiveOIBench: Can Large Language Models Outperform Human Contestants in Informatics Olympiads?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A3</span></a></p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table A3: </span>Problem difficulty distribution using quantile thresholds.</figcaption>
<table id="A1.T3.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="A1.T3.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A1.T3.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt"><span id="A1.T3.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Level</span></td>
<td id="A1.T3.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_tt"><span id="A1.T3.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"># Problems</span></td>
<td id="A1.T3.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_tt"><span id="A1.T3.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">% of Total</span></td>
<td id="A1.T3.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_tt"><span id="A1.T3.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Threshold Rule</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A1.T3.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A1.T3.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">Easy</td>
<td id="A1.T3.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_t">143</td>
<td id="A1.T3.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_t">35.48%</td>
